1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,800
In 2023, scientists took a lump of mud
from 16 metres under a lake in Siberia.

2
00:00:06,500 --> 00:00:11,000
They thawed it out in a lab, and a
virus inside it was still infectious.

3
00:00:11,938 --> 00:00:15,338
It had been frozen for
more than 48,000 years.

4
00:00:16,038 --> 00:00:19,898
And it could still infect a living
cell: an amoeba, not a human.

5
00:00:20,484 --> 00:00:22,224
The ground it came from is thawing.

6
00:00:22,304 --> 00:00:24,544
So what else is in there?

7
00:00:25,004 --> 00:00:27,044
Diseases that vanished long ago?

8
00:00:27,124 --> 00:00:30,304
Or something that could
make the planet even warmer?

9
00:00:30,954 --> 00:00:32,954
Some of this is already happening.

10
00:00:33,214 --> 00:00:37,314
But the biggest danger in that ground
may not be the one you're expecting.

11
00:00:39,314 --> 00:00:42,674
The frozen ground is called
permafrost: ground that stays

12
00:00:42,904 --> 00:00:45,854
at or below freezing for at
least two years in a row.

13
00:00:46,450 --> 00:00:50,230
It lies under about 15 per cent of
the land in the Northern Hemisphere.

14
00:00:50,950 --> 00:00:55,850
Siberia, Alaska, northern Canada,
the ice-free edges of Greenland.

15
00:00:56,660 --> 00:00:58,140
And it isn't just ice.

16
00:00:58,940 --> 00:01:03,460
It's soil, rock and frozen water, packed
with the remains of plants and animals.

17
00:01:04,182 --> 00:01:07,242
In places, it reaches down
more than a kilometre.

18
00:01:07,322 --> 00:01:11,122
And some of it formed hundreds
of thousands of years ago.

19
00:01:11,704 --> 00:01:13,884
Here's what happens when it thaws.

20
00:01:14,444 --> 00:01:16,884
This is Batagaika, in Yakutia.

21
00:01:17,044 --> 00:01:21,704
In the 1960s, it was a gully across
a hillside stripped of its forest.

22
00:01:22,471 --> 00:01:24,891
Today, it's a crater
almost two kilometres long.

23
00:01:25,121 --> 00:01:30,631
And its walls are still crumbling back,
on average about ten metres a year.

24
00:01:31,166 --> 00:01:35,646
Without the ice holding it together,
the ground simply slumps and collapses.

25
00:01:36,686 --> 00:01:40,106
And whatever was frozen inside
meets air and water again.

26
00:01:40,651 --> 00:01:44,131
By the way, if you like videos
like this, consider subscribing.

27
00:01:44,751 --> 00:01:46,351
It really helps the channel.

28
00:01:48,741 --> 00:01:51,021
So let's start with the scary question.

29
00:01:51,741 --> 00:01:53,641
Could something in there make us sick?

30
00:01:54,321 --> 00:01:57,221
That virus from 2023 wasn't the first.

31
00:01:58,241 --> 00:02:01,661
In 2014, the same team in
France revived one from

32
00:02:01,891 --> 00:02:05,701
northeast Siberia that was
about 30,000 years old.

33
00:02:06,281 --> 00:02:08,661
By now, they've revived about a dozen.

34
00:02:09,361 --> 00:02:12,781
And every single one of them
infects amoebas, not people.

35
00:02:13,419 --> 00:02:17,679
That's on purpose. Amoebas are
the bait: they let you fish out

36
00:02:17,909 --> 00:02:20,679
ancient viruses without
risking human ones.

37
00:02:21,167 --> 00:02:23,427
So yes, something frozen
for tens of thousands

38
00:02:23,657 --> 00:02:26,207
of years can still infect a living cell.

39
00:02:26,927 --> 00:02:29,407
But an amoeba virus isn't a human disease.

40
00:02:32,167 --> 00:02:33,887
For something that can
kill people, we have

41
00:02:34,117 --> 00:02:39,747
to go to Yamal, a peninsula in
Arctic Russia. Summer, 2016.

42
00:02:40,436 --> 00:02:42,756
After years of warming,
capped by a heatwave,

43
00:02:42,836 --> 00:02:44,996
the ground had thawed deeper than usual.

44
00:02:45,856 --> 00:02:47,196
Then the reindeer started dying.

45
00:02:47,426 --> 00:02:48,956
Of anthrax.

46
00:02:49,665 --> 00:02:50,945
More than 2,000 of them.

47
00:02:51,175 --> 00:02:56,165
Thirty-six people were infected,
and a 12-year-old boy died.

48
00:02:56,867 --> 00:03:00,067
Anthrax spores can survive
in the soil for decades.

49
00:03:00,947 --> 00:03:04,207
The last outbreak in the
area had been in 1941.

50
00:03:05,050 --> 00:03:08,190
The leading explanation: the
thaw reached old infected

51
00:03:08,420 --> 00:03:11,490
carcasses, and the spores
came back to the surface.

52
00:03:11,986 --> 00:03:14,056
But it wasn't only the thaw.

53
00:03:14,226 --> 00:03:18,826
Vaccination had stopped in 2007,
and the herds had grown huge.

54
00:03:19,546 --> 00:03:21,946
The thaw was the trigger,
not the whole cause.

55
00:03:22,667 --> 00:03:24,927
Scientists do take this seriously.

56
00:03:25,767 --> 00:03:28,567
Nobody knows everything
that's frozen in there.

57
00:03:29,087 --> 00:03:31,387
But there's no evidence
that a wave of prehistoric

58
00:03:31,617 --> 00:03:34,307
diseases is about to
come out of the Arctic.

59
00:03:37,117 --> 00:03:39,637
The bigger danger isn't
something waking up.

60
00:03:40,037 --> 00:03:41,697
It's something breaking down.

61
00:03:43,957 --> 00:03:46,597
For thousands of years,
Arctic plants grew every

62
00:03:46,827 --> 00:03:50,357
summer, pulled carbon dioxide
out of the air, and died.

63
00:03:50,987 --> 00:03:52,987
In the cold, they barely rotted.

64
00:03:53,367 --> 00:03:57,147
Year after year, the carbon piled
up and froze into the ground.

65
00:03:57,647 --> 00:03:59,527
So how much is down there?

66
00:03:59,987 --> 00:04:02,627
Let's compare it with
something we already know.

67
00:04:03,249 --> 00:04:07,669
Since 1850, by burning coal,
oil and gas, humanity has

68
00:04:07,899 --> 00:04:11,129
put about 500 billion tonnes
of carbon into the air.

69
00:04:11,887 --> 00:04:15,727
The soils of the permafrost region
hold about three times as much.

70
00:04:17,597 --> 00:04:19,837
Around 1.5 trillion tonnes.

71
00:04:19,917 --> 00:04:23,357
More than the entire
atmosphere holds today.

72
00:04:23,945 --> 00:04:26,205
More than half of it is frozen solid.

73
00:04:26,805 --> 00:04:30,265
The rest lies in soil that
thaws only briefly each summer.

74
00:04:30,965 --> 00:04:33,625
Either way, it's the
cold that keeps it there.

75
00:04:34,179 --> 00:04:35,739
It won't all come out.

76
00:04:36,499 --> 00:04:39,319
If emissions keep climbing,
scientists estimate that

77
00:04:39,549 --> 00:04:42,959
perhaps 5 to 15 per cent of
it could escape this century.

78
00:04:43,578 --> 00:04:47,438
That sounds small. But 10 per
cent of that pile is more than a

79
00:04:47,668 --> 00:04:50,118
quarter of everything we've burned so far.

80
00:04:52,668 --> 00:04:56,748
When the ground thaws, ordinary
soil microbes finally get to eat.

81
00:04:57,372 --> 00:05:01,892
Where there's plenty of oxygen, they
turn that old carbon into carbon dioxide.

82
00:05:03,092 --> 00:05:05,732
In waterlogged ground,
partly into methane.

83
00:05:06,537 --> 00:05:09,857
Methane traps about 80 times
more heat than carbon dioxide

84
00:05:10,087 --> 00:05:14,057
over 20 years, though it
breaks down in about a decade.

85
00:05:14,782 --> 00:05:17,102
Either way, the result is more heat.

86
00:05:17,782 --> 00:05:21,002
And more heat means more thawing,
which releases more carbon.

87
00:05:21,622 --> 00:05:23,942
It's called the permafrost
carbon feedback.

88
00:05:24,781 --> 00:05:26,481
It won't happen all at once.

89
00:05:27,161 --> 00:05:30,081
It's slow, uneven, and
different from place to place.

90
00:05:30,509 --> 00:05:34,689
But once that carbon is in the air, we
can't freeze it back into the ground.

91
00:05:35,763 --> 00:05:39,043
And there's a much faster way to
get carbon out of the ground. Fire.

92
00:05:40,701 --> 00:05:45,821
In 2007, lightning set fire to the
tundra of Alaska's North Slope.

93
00:05:46,741 --> 00:05:50,121
The Anaktuvuk River fire burned
more than 1,000 square kilometres.

94
00:05:51,402 --> 00:05:56,422
In that spot, the lake sediments show
no sign of fire for about 5,000 years.

95
00:05:56,823 --> 00:05:59,763
And that single fire released
about as much carbon as

96
00:05:59,993 --> 00:06:03,843
the entire Arctic tundra used
to absorb in a whole year.

97
00:06:04,343 --> 00:06:09,063
The fire also burned away the plants
and peat that kept the ground insulated.

98
00:06:09,823 --> 00:06:14,023
Within seven years, a third of the
burned area had started to sink.

99
00:06:14,639 --> 00:06:16,819
And Arctic fires are getting bigger.

100
00:06:17,619 --> 00:06:22,339
In 2020, fires inside the Arctic
Circle broke the record for emissions.

101
00:06:23,249 --> 00:06:25,109
Some of them don't even go out.

102
00:06:25,589 --> 00:06:29,369
They smoulder underground all
winter and flare up again in spring.

103
00:06:29,909 --> 00:06:31,729
They're called zombie fires.

104
00:06:34,189 --> 00:06:35,709
Now put it all together.

105
00:06:36,109 --> 00:06:39,069
The thaw, the microbes, the fires.

106
00:06:39,857 --> 00:06:43,557
For thousands of years, the Arctic
tundra did one thing with carbon.

107
00:06:43,677 --> 00:06:45,337
It stored it.

108
00:06:45,886 --> 00:06:48,846
In 2024, the yearly Arctic report from

109
00:06:49,076 --> 00:06:51,666
NOAA concluded that
this is no longer true.

110
00:06:52,254 --> 00:06:54,834
Once the fires are counted, the tundra now

111
00:06:55,064 --> 00:06:58,014
releases more carbon
dioxide than it takes in.

112
00:06:58,537 --> 00:07:00,117
Only a little, for now.

113
00:07:00,717 --> 00:07:03,997
The forests further south still
absorb more than they lose.

114
00:07:04,715 --> 00:07:06,315
But the ground that
spent millennia pulling

115
00:07:06,545 --> 00:07:09,295
carbon out of the air has
started giving it back.

116
00:07:12,835 --> 00:07:16,055
For the people who live up there,
this isn't a future problem.

117
00:07:16,695 --> 00:07:19,235
The ground under their
homes is already moving.

118
00:07:19,887 --> 00:07:24,507
By 2050, about 70 per cent of the
roads, buildings and pipelines on

119
00:07:24,737 --> 00:07:28,187
permafrost will stand on ground
that could thaw near the surface.

120
00:07:30,867 --> 00:07:33,167
So what happens when the world thaws?

121
00:07:33,287 --> 00:07:35,767
Probably not a prehistoric plague.

122
00:07:36,687 --> 00:07:39,587
The bigger danger is a vast
store of carbon, kept out

123
00:07:39,817 --> 00:07:42,747
of the atmosphere by the
cold, leaking back into it.

124
00:07:43,277 --> 00:07:45,637
How much warming it will
add is still uncertain.

125
00:07:46,617 --> 00:07:48,617
But uncertain doesn't mean small.

126
00:07:49,305 --> 00:07:52,345
The greatest danger in the
ice isn't something waking up.

127
00:07:52,425 --> 00:07:54,685
It's something breaking down.

128
00:07:55,245 --> 00:07:58,115
And the less we warm the Arctic,
the more of it stays frozen.
